// The section "Generics" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp Generics() {
    return <>
        <p>
            Type parameters go in angle brackets, with a constraint after each, as in Go:
            {" "}<code>{"func last<T any>(xs T[]) ?T"}</code>, <code>{"interface Box<T>"}</code>,
            {" "}<code>{"class Stack<T>"}</code>. Type arguments are in angle brackets too:
            {" "}<code>{"Box<number>"}</code>, <code>{"Stack<string>"}</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The compiler infers the type arguments of a call from the arguments or from the
            expected type; a call may also give them, <code>{"fill<Song>(2)"}</code>. The
            constraint <code>{"<T Named>"}</code> allows only fitting types and gives values of type
            {" "}<code>T</code> the members of <code>Named</code>. In JS the types are erased.
        </p>
        <Example name="generics" />
        <p>
            A generic function may make a value of its type, <code>make(T)</code>, or check a value
            against it, <code>{"decode<T>"}</code>: T then comes from each call
            (<a href="../make/">make</a>).
        </p>
    </>
}
